  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday night through Tuesday)\nIssued at 233 AM EDT Wed Aug 5 2026\n\nFor the end of the week, the main feature of focus will be the \napproach of a mid level and surface inverted trough that will be \nmoving towards the region from the western Atlantic. This feature \nwill allow for the pressure gradient to remain tight over South \nFlorida on Friday resulting in a breezy east to southeasterly wind \nflow to remain in place. Uncertainty does rise for this part of \nthe forecast as the latest global and ensemble guidance suite \nremains in disagreement in regards to how it handles the intensity\nand mid level energy associated with this inverted trough. The \nECMWF guidance suite shows the bulk of the mid level energy \nassociated with this disturbance curving offshore before reaching \nthe Florida coastline, however, the GFS guidance suite pushes most\nof the mid level energy over the region during this time frame. \nThe recurved/more offshore solution would keep the higher moisture\nsurge offshore and it would feature more of a typical summertime \npattern with showers and storms that are more sea breeze driven. \nWith the extra mid to upper level support pushing over the Florida\nPeninsula, this would help to enhance shower and thunderstorm \ncoverage during this time frame. Given the uncertainty, the \nlatest forecast does continue to favor elevated shower and \nthunderstorm chances on Friday, however, it keeps the highest \nchances over the interior as well as Southwest Florida due to the \nstronger easterly wind flow. This will continue to be monitored \nas the week progresses. High temperatures on Friday will generally\nrise into the upper 80s and lower 90s with peak heat index values\nranging between 103 and 108. \n\nDuring the upcoming weekend, this inverted trough will quickly push \noff to the north of the region on Friday night and rather strong mid \nlevel ridging will begin to extend southwestward again from the \nwestern Atlantic across South Florida on Saturday and Sunday. \nSurface high pressure centered in the western Atlantic will also \nextend southwestward back towards the region and into the eastern \nGulf during this time frame. This will result in a return to \nconvective initiation being sea breeze driven and with east to \nsoutheasterly wind flow staying in place, the highest chances of \nshowers and storms will remain over the interior and Southwest \nFlorida during the afternoon and evening hours. Due to mid level \nridging building back into the area, this will allow for high \ntemperatures to return to the lower 90s across most areas with peak \nheat index values generally ranging between 105 and 110 each day \nduring this time frame. \n\nHeading into the early portion of next week, the latest global and \nensemble guidance continues to hint at strong mid level ridging over \nthe western Atlantic continuing to nudge closer to South Florida \nheading into Monday. However, there is also a signal of another mid \nlevel inverted trough that may try to push towards the region \nheading into Tuesday. Uncertainty in this part of the forecast is\non the higher end due to guidance discrepancies in the exact \nlocation and strength of this feature. This part of the forecast \nmay also be complicated by the potential arrival of a Saharan Dust\nlayer that will be approaching the region as well from the \nsoutheast. For now, the latest forecast takes a blend of the \nforecast models and features sea breeze initiated convection each \nday with the highest chances remaining over the interior and \nSouthwest Florida. High temperatures during the early portion of \nthe week have the potential to rise above climatological normals \ndue to the stronger ridging in place and the potential Saharan \nDust pushing into the region.",
